entries.body:
Acronym for “*Bitcoin Improvement Proposal*”. A BIP is a formal document proposing an improvement, a technical specification or information relating to the Bitcoin protocol and its ecosystem. Since Bitcoin has no central entity to decide on updates, BIPs allow the community to suggest, discuss and implement improvements in a structured and transparent way.

Each BIP details the objectives of the proposed improvement, the rationale, the potential impacts on compatibility, as well as the advantages and drawbacks. BIPs can be written by any member of the community. They are primarily owned by their authors and represent their personal recommendation: an individual BIP does not constitute community consensus or a general implementation recommendation.

The editors who maintain the BIPs GitHub repository are: Bryan Bishop, Jon Atack, Luke Dashjr, Mark Erhardt (Murch), Olaoluwa Osuntokun and Ruben Somsen. Their role is administrative and editorial: they check the formatting, relevance and quality of proposals, but do not assess whether a proposal will be adopted. If someone proposes an improvement that is not accepted within the formal BIP framework, they can still present it directly to the Bitcoin community, or even create a fork including their modification. The advantage of the BIP process lies in its formality and centralization, which facilitate debate in order to avoid division among Bitcoin users, by seeking to implement updates by consensus. In the end, it is indeed the principle of economic majority that determines the power dynamics within the protocol.

BIPs are classified into three types (since BIP-0003, which replaced BIP-0002 in 2025):
* `Specification BIPs` (formerly “Standards Track”): Define a set of technical rules describing a new feature or affecting the interoperability of implementations. They can be implemented, and implementations can conform to their specification. They must contain a reference implementation and test vectors before reaching “Complete” status;
* `Informational BIPs`: Describe a design issue, provide general recommendations or information useful to the Bitcoin community;
* `Process BIPs`: Describe a process surrounding Bitcoin or propose a change to a process. They are treated as living documents, modifiable indefinitely by rough consensus on the mailing list.

“Specification” and “Informational” BIPs can be classified by layer (“Layer”):
* `Consensus (soft fork)` and `Consensus (hard fork)`: Concern Bitcoin's consensus rules, such as modifications to block or transaction validation rules. For example, the SegWit and Taproot BIPs belong to this category;
* `Peer Services`: Concerns the operation of the Bitcoin node network, that is, how nodes find and communicate with each other over the Internet;
* `API/RPC`: Concerns the application programming interfaces (APIs) and remote procedure calls (RPCs) that allow Bitcoin software to interact with nodes;
* `Applications`: Concerns applications built on top of Bitcoin. BIPs in this category typically address changes at the level of Bitcoin wallet standards.

The BIP submission process begins with conceptualizing and discussing the idea on the *Bitcoin Development Mailing List*. Authors must first verify that the idea has not already been proposed, then present it publicly to gather initial feedback. If the idea is promising and has generated sufficient interest in the community, the author writes a BIP following a specific format (MediaWiki or Markdown) and submits it via a Pull Request on the BIPs GitHub repository. The editors verify that the proposal complies with formatting, is relevant, has been previously discussed on the mailing list, and is technically feasible. A number is assigned by an editor only after the proposal has generated tangible interest in the community. Authors can also appoint “Deputies”, co-owners of the BIP who assist them in the process.

BIPs go through four statuses during their lifecycle (simplified by BIP-0003 from the nine statuses of BIP-0002):
* Draft (`Draft`): The proposal is being written and discussed. A BIP in “Draft” with no activity for one year may be closed if its authors do not show intent to continue;
* Complete (`Complete`): The authors have finished the intended work, the proposal is clear, complete and ready for adoption. “Specification” BIPs must include a reference implementation and test vectors. After this status, changes must remain minor. This is the final status of most successful informational BIPs;
* Deployed (`Deployed`): The BIP is actively used in the ecosystem. This status is reached upon presentation of evidence of actual use (production deployment by an established project, soft fork activation criteria met, etc.). Once deployed, the BIP is considered final and should no longer undergo substantial modifications;
* Closed (`Closed`): The BIP is no longer actively developed or used and is of historical interest only. This status replaces the former statuses “Deferred”, “Rejected”, “Withdrawn”, “Replaced” and “Obsolete”.

After reaching “Complete” status, any change must be documented in a “Changelog” section and versioned according to a semantic scheme.

Document that defines the process for developing and implementing improvements proposed to the Bitcoin protocol. Written by Amir Taaki in September 2011, it establishes a standard methodology for proposing and documenting potential changes. BIP proposals are then classified into three categories: 
* `Standards Track`: those that concern direct modifications of the Bitcoin protocol and its interoperability;
* `Informational`: those that offer general information, but do not directly affect the protocol; 
* `Process`: those that introduce non-technical changes, such as procedures and guidelines.

This framework systematizes the development of Bitcoin, in order to ensure a coordinated and transparent approach to its evolution. The BIP-0001 methodology would later be replaced by that of BIP-0002.

Developed following the debates on SegWit, which used BIP-0009 for its activation, BIP-0008 is a soft fork activation method that natively incorporates an automatic UASF (*User-Activated Soft Fork*) mechanism. Like BIP-0009, BIP-0008 uses miner signaling, but adds the parameter `LOT` (*Lock-in On Time out*). If `LOT` is set to `vrai`, when the signaling period expires without reaching the required threshold, a UASF is automatically triggered, forcing the activation of the soft fork. This approach forces miners to be cooperative or risk a UASF imposed by users. Moreover, unlike BIP-0009, BIP-0008 defines the signaling period based on block height, eliminating potential manipulation by miners via hash rate. BIP-0008 also allows a variable voting threshold to be set and introduces a parameter for a minimum block height for activation, giving miners time to prepare and signal their agreement in advance without necessarily being ready. When a soft fork is activated via BIP-0008 with the parameter `LOT=vrai`, a very aggressive method is used against miners, who are immediately put under the pressure of a possible UASF. Indeed, this leaves them with only 2 choices:
* Be cooperative, and thus facilitate the activation process;
* Be uncooperative, in which case users automatically perform a UASF to impose the soft fork.

Improvement proposal that introduces a standard hierarchical derivation structure for HD wallets. BIP-0044 builds on the principles established by BIP-0032 for key derivation and on BIP-0043 for the use of the “*purpose*” field. It introduces a structure of five derivation levels: `m / purpose' / coin_type' / account' / change / address_index`. Here is the detail of each depth:
* `m /` indicates the master private key. It is unique to a wallet and cannot have siblings at the same depth. The master key is derived directly from the wallet's seed;
* `m / purpose' /` indicates the derivation purpose, which makes it possible to identify the standard followed. This field is described in BIP-0043. For example, if the wallet follows the BIP-0084 standard (SegWit V0), the index will then be `84'`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' /` indicates the type of cryptocurrency. This makes it possible to clearly distinguish the branches dedicated to one cryptocurrency from the branches dedicated to another cryptocurrency on a multi-coin wallet. The index dedicated to Bitcoin is `0'`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' /` indicates the account number. This depth makes it possible to easily differentiate and organize a wallet into different accounts. These accounts are numbered starting from `0'`. The extended keys (`xpub`, `xprv`...) are found at this depth level;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' / change /` indicates the chain. Each account as defined at depth 3 has two chains at depth 4: an external chain and an internal chain (also called “*change*”). The external chain derives addresses intended to be shared publicly, that is, the addresses offered to us when we click on “receive” in our wallet software. The internal chain derives addresses intended not to be shared publicly, that is, mainly change addresses. The external chain is identified with index `0` and the internal chain is identified with index `1`. You will notice that from this depth onward, we no longer perform a hardened derivation, but a normal derivation (there is no apostrophe). It is thanks to this mechanism that we are able to derive all the child public keys from their `xpub`;
* `m / purpose' / coin-type' / account' / change / address-index` simply indicates the number of the receiving address and its key pair, in order to differentiate it from its siblings at the same depth on the same branch. For example, the first derived address has index `0`, the second address has index `1`, etc...

For example, if my receiving address has the derivation path `m / 86' / 0' / 0' / 0 / 5`, we can deduce the following information:
* `86'` indicates that we are following the BIP-0086 derivation standard (Taproot or SegWit V1);
* `0'` indicates that it is a Bitcoin address;
* `0'` indicates that we are on the first account of the wallet;
* `0` indicates that it is an external address;
* `5` indicates that it is the sixth external address of this account.

![BIP-84 tree illustrating the five-level BIP-44 structure: purpose, coin type, accounts, chains and addresses.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/b/bip-0044/assets/image-1.png)

Establishes a new standardized process for the classification of Bitcoin improvement proposals. BIPs must from now on be classified according to 4 layers (*layers*):
* `Consensus`: concerns proposals that require a consensus change and affect compatibility between earlier and future versions of the Bitcoin protocol. These are soft forks and hard forks;
* `Peer Services`: concerns changes to the services and communication protocols between network nodes, without affecting consensus;
* `API/RPC`: encompasses proposals aiming to modify the APIs and RPCs used to interact with Bitcoin nodes;
* `Applications`: includes proposals for improvements to applications that run on top of the Bitcoin network, such as typically the standards related to wallet software.

Introduces the bech32 address format for SegWit V0 addresses. This address format is characterized by the prefix `bc1q`. The bech32 format offers several advantages: 
* It requires less space in QR codes;
* It is more easily interpretable by humans;
* It has an innovative checksum mechanism that performs better and makes it possible to detect and potentially automatically correct typing errors.
These characteristics make receiving addresses easier to use while minimizing the risk of errors.

Bitcoin is the name of the peer-to-peer electronic cash system created by Satoshi Nakamoto in 2009. Using the term Bitcoin with a capital "B" can refer to three different things:
* The Bitcoin system;
* The Bitcoin protocol;
* The Bitcoin network.

The term bitcoin with a lowercase "b" is generally reserved for the monetary unit exchanged on this system.

![Bitcoin emblem with the capital B, used to refer to the system, the protocol or the network.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/b/bitcoin-b-majuscule/assets/image-1.png)

Data structure in the Bitcoin system. A block contains a set of valid transactions and metadata contained in its header. Each block is linked to the next by the hash of its header, thus forming the blockchain (chain of blocks). The blockchain acts as a timestamp server that allows each user to know all past transactions, in order to verify the non-existence of a transaction and prevent double spending. Transactions are organized in a Merkle tree. This cryptographic accumulator makes it possible to produce a digest of all the transactions in a block, called the “Merkle root” (*Merkle root*). The header of a block contains 6 elements:
* The block version;
* The hash of the previous block;
* The root of the transactions' Merkle tree;
* The block timestamp;
* The difficulty target;
* The nonce.

To be valid, a block must have a header that, once hashed with `SHA256d`, produces a digest less than or equal to the difficulty target.
